On Wed, 24 Jan 2007, Gary Olson wrote:
> DarthBob88 wrote:
>> This is a conduct especially for Marvin, the More Dread Lord: Ascending
>> with one hand tied behind back. Presumably, it would include fighting
>> without using #twoweapon, using one-handed tools and weapons only, and
>> not using any spells or scrolls which require both hands to cast or
>> read. Naturally, this will never be coded, but it would be cool to see
>> "Conducts kept: Ascended with one hand tied behind back." Any further
>> ideas?
>> 
> 1) the time to put your boots on would be 2.5x the normal number of turns. 
> (go ahead try it, test yourself, put your boots on with just one hand)  Same 
> for gloves

   No gloves. They only come in pairs, and you certainly couldn't put on
the left glove if your left hand was tied behind your back. (Nor could
you put on the right glove, really; try it?)

> 2) Try not to fall into your bag of holding...without that other hand to hold 
> it open, retrieving items could get tricky.

   No 'a'pplying containers from inventory; you must drop and #loot them
instead.

> 3) How are you going to light that FIRST candle with only one hand?

   ...And no matches? ;)  If the candle's standing upright on the ground,
or in the Candelabrum, and you hold the matchbox in your teeth, you can
light it. I think lighting should be allowed.

> 4) No bows allowed....unless you have a really long neck and some strong 
> teeth

   Of course.

> 5) Does it take one hand or two to sacrifice?

   I think saccing would be allowed. However, since $deity-granted items
appear "in your hands" plural, you must not sacrifice if there's a chance
you'll be granted a two-handed weapon. (I have no idea how much of a
restriction that is.) Fair?

> 6) How will you rub a touchstone to a gem with only one hand?

   Teeth.

> 7) Can you do alchemy with only one hand?

   Dunno. #dip in general will be problematic with only one hand, I think, 
except for #dipping in fountains; shall we say no dipping?

> 8) Higher probability of failing to write a scroll or spellbook with one 
> hand. HOlding down that parchment with your feet could be difficult.

Aw, come on. You just lie down with the paper in front of you and write;
the pressure of your forearm, hand, and marker keeps it steady. It's
been too long since you did homework in that posture, obviously. ;)

> 9) Digging with a pickaxe would be 3x slower with only one hand; and 
> impossible with 2 handed implements.

   I don't know why a pick-axe isn't two-handed, honestly. But if this
is a conduct, as opposed to a patch, you can't say "digging would be
slower"; digging is what it is.

> 10) Can you wield a weapon and use Touch of Death with one hand permanently 
> disabled?

   I believe it's "Finger of Death", and merely involves pointing,
which you can do while still maintaining your grip on the hilt with
your other four fingers.


   Greasing things would be hard, but I'd hesitate to pronounce it
"impossible". Riding and using a weapon at the same time should break
the conduct, except /maybe/ if you're expert in riding skill.
Intentionally polymorphing into anything limbless breaks the conduct.

